## ⚡ 1. Why Agentic IR Breaks the Standard Playbook

Five structural differences. Each one invalidates a step most IR plans depend on.

| # | Standard IR assumption | Agentic reality | Consequence |
|---|------------------------|-----------------|-------------|
| 1 | **Compromise = unauthorised access** | Every action used valid credentials and permitted operations | Access logs show nothing anomalous. Detection must key on *intent deviation*, not authorisation |
| 2 | **Containment = isolate the host** | The agent's harmful capability is its *granted scope*, spread across SaaS APIs and peer agents | Isolating compute doesn't revoke authority. Containment is an identity and policy action |
| 3 | **Eradication = remove the artefact** | The attacker's instruction may now be a *belief* in persistent memory | There is no file to delete. Eradication means memory forensics and selective rollback |
| 4 | **Blast radius grows at human speed** | Fan-out is machine-speed and multi-hop across an agent mesh | Response time budget is minutes, not hours. Manual triage arrives after the damage |
| 5 | **Root cause is in your estate** | Trigger may be a sentence inside a third party's document, read six weeks ago | Root cause analysis requires provenance lineage you either captured or didn't |

> **The load-bearing consequence:** if you cannot reconstruct *which input span caused which tool call*, you cannot close an agentic incident — you can only stop it. Every runbook below depends on the telemetry from [Day 54 §5](../Day54). Without it, your best case is containment without eradication, which means recurrence.

