## 🚨 2. Severity Taxonomy

Standard sev scales key on data volume and system criticality. Agentic severity must also key on **autonomy** and **reversibility** — an agent with write access and no human gate is a fundamentally different incident from a read-only copilot.

| Sev | Criteria (any one) | Response | Exec notification |
|-----|--------------------|----------|-------------------|
| **AI-SEV1** | Agent took irreversible action on production/financial/customer systems · confirmed exfiltration via agent tooling · rogue behaviour with live credentials · cascade affecting >1 business service | Immediate fleet-level containment. War room. | CISO + GC immediately; board within 24h |
| **AI-SEV2** | Confirmed goal hijack with tool execution · memory poisoning in a shared/multi-tenant store · privilege escalation via delegation chain · cascade contained to one service | Agent-level containment within 30 min. IR lead assigned. | CISO within 1h |
| **AI-SEV3** | Injection attempt reached the context window but policy denied the action · single-tenant memory poisoning · anomalous tool chain, no confirmed impact | Investigate within 4h. Policy tuning. | Weekly governance forum |
| **AI-SEV4** | Guardrail fired as designed · drift within tolerance · near-miss with no action attempted | Log, trend, feed detection engineering | Monthly metrics |

**Two escalation rules that matter:**

- **Autonomy multiplier** — any incident on an agent with write scope *and* no human-in-the-loop gate escalates one level automatically. The absence of a gate is itself an aggravating factor.
- **Multi-tenant memory rule** — poisoning of a memory store shared across tenants or business units is never below SEV2, regardless of apparent impact, because dwell time is unbounded until proven otherwise.

